{
  "image_name": "0c877f5e3ac49e5a664bd414271a2d87.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure gives a broad overview of experimental and computational approaches used to understand electrochemical reactions at catalyst–electrolyte interfaces, with a “future outlook” framing. Across the image, the central theme is that performance depends on coupled interfacial processes such as charge transfer at the surface and mass transport between the surface and bulk electrolyte.\n\nIt highlights several complementary methods. Scanning probe microscopy is shown as a way to map surfaces with high spatial resolution, follow changes in real time, and locate active sites. Operando electron microscopy is presented as a liquid-cell approach that can visualize catalysts between electrodes under an electron beam, providing atomic-scale and dynamic structural information, even down to single-particle behavior. An electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance is included to monitor mass changes during reaction, helping quantify adsorption/desorption and assess catalyst stability or degradation. Another panel shows operando studies in flow cells and membrane electrode assemblies, emphasizing more realistic device conditions, mass transport, and electrode–membrane interfacial behavior.\n\nThe figure also connects experiments with kinetic analysis and theory, including reaction kinetics, activation energy, reaction pathways, computation, and AI. Overall, it conveys that combining operando tools across scales is essential for understanding how electrochemical interfaces evolve and function.",
